From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 10:18:06 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] xen/dom0less: seed next_phandle below the reserved range

a010efd323 ("xen/dom0less: introduce next_phandle in struct kernel_info")
seeds kinfo->next_phandle with fdt_generate_phandle(), which returns one
past the *highest* phandle in the partial FDT, and then refuses the domain
when that value falls inside the Xen-reserved range:

    if ( kinfo->next_phandle >= GUEST_PHANDLE_GIC )
        res = -EOVERFLOW;

Every partial FDT written for the documented passthrough idiom trips this.
The dummy /gic placeholder is expected to carry GUEST_PHANDLE_GIC (65000):

  * libxl gives the GIC node it generates for an xl-created guest exactly
    that value (tools/libs/light/libxl_arm.c: make_gicv{2,3}_node()), so a
    fragment shared between `xl create device_tree=` and dom0less has to use
    it or its interrupt-parent references dangle;
  * dom0less itself used the same constant unconditionally up to 4.21 --
    prepare_dtb_domU() set kinfo->phandle_intc = GUEST_PHANDLE_GIC and only
    then let init_intc_phandle() override it from the fragment -- so every
    existing fragment was written against it;
  * a010efd323's own commit message says the pfdt "may already contain a
    dummy interrupt controller node with a phandle assigned to it".

fdt_generate_phandle() therefore returns GUEST_PHANDLE_GIC + 1, which is
GUEST_PHANDLE_IOMMU, and the domain is rejected before any node is copied.
On a dom0less system that is fatal: construct_domU() fails, the toolstack
domain is never built, and Xen panics with

    (XEN) Device tree generation failed (-75).
    (XEN) Panic on CPU 0: Could not set up domain domD (rc = -22)

The purpose of next_phandle is to let Xen allocate phandles for the nodes
it generates without colliding with the fragment's own, and alloc_phandle()
must keep those below the reserved range. A phandle the fragment merely
declares in that range does not constrain that: what matters is the highest
phandle Xen must not reuse *among the values it may allocate*. So seed
next_phandle from the highest phandle strictly below GUEST_PHANDLE_GIC,
which leaves the guarantee intact while accepting the reserved placeholder.

The >= GUEST_PHANDLE_GIC check is kept: a fragment that really has consumed
the phandle space right up to the reserved range still has to be refused,
because alloc_phandle() would have nothing left to hand out.

Scope note for review: this ignores every phandle at or above the limit, not
just the intc placeholder's. A fragment that put a reserved-range phandle on
some other node would be accepted here where upstream refused it; if that node
is copied into the guest DT (only /aliases and /passthrough are), its phandle
could then collide with the vGIC node Xen generates at GUEST_PHANDLE_GIC. The
fragments in this workspace do not do that -- the rpi4 DomD partial FDT carries
65000 on /gic and 1..16 under /passthrough, nothing else -- but narrowing the
exemption to the node init_intc_phandle() accepts would be the stricter form,
and is the shape to propose if this goes to xen-devel.

Upstream-Status: Pending
Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-opus-5
Signed-off-by: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
---
 xen/common/device-tree/dom0less-build.c | 44 +++++++++++++++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 36 insertions(+), 8 deletions(-)

--- a/xen/common/device-tree/dom0less-build.c
+++ b/xen/common/device-tree/dom0less-build.c
@@ -388,6 +388,33 @@
     return 0;
 }
 
+/*
+ * Highest phandle in the partial FDT that is strictly below @limit, or 0 when
+ * the FDT declares none. Values at or above @limit are ignored on purpose: the
+ * documented passthrough idiom has the dummy /gic placeholder carry
+ * GUEST_PHANDLE_GIC, and libxl gives the GIC node it generates for xl-created
+ * guests the same value, so a fragment usable from both paths always declares
+ * one. Those phandles are never handed out by alloc_phandle(), so they do not
+ * constrain where Xen's own allocations have to start.
+ */
+static uint32_t __init pfdt_max_phandle_below(const void *pfdt, uint32_t limit)
+{
+    uint32_t max = 0;
+    int offset;
+
+    for ( offset = fdt_next_node(pfdt, -1, NULL);
+          offset >= 0;
+          offset = fdt_next_node(pfdt, offset, NULL) )
+    {
+        uint32_t phandle = fdt_get_phandle(pfdt, offset);
+
+        if ( phandle && phandle < limit && phandle > max )
+            max = phandle;
+    }
+
+    return max;
+}
+
 static int __init domain_handle_dtb_boot_module(struct domain *d,
                                                 struct kernel_info *kinfo)
 {
@@ -403,16 +430,17 @@
         goto out;
 
     /*
-     * Find the highest phandle in the partial FDT so next_phandle starts
-     * above it, avoiding collisions with pfdt's own phandle assignments.
+     * Find the highest phandle below the Xen-reserved range so next_phandle
+     * starts above it, avoiding collisions with the pfdt's own assignments
+     * without rejecting the reserved GUEST_PHANDLE_GIC that the dummy
+     * interrupt controller placeholder is expected to carry.
      */
-    res = fdt_generate_phandle(pfdt, &kinfo->next_phandle);
-    if ( res )
-    {
-        res = (res == -FDT_ERR_NOPHANDLES) ? -EOVERFLOW : -EINVAL;
-        goto out;
-    }
+    kinfo->next_phandle = pfdt_max_phandle_below(pfdt, GUEST_PHANDLE_GIC) + 1;
 
+    /*
+     * Only reachable when the fragment has consumed the phandle space right up
+     * to the reserved range, leaving alloc_phandle() nothing to hand out.
+     */
     if ( kinfo->next_phandle >= GUEST_PHANDLE_GIC )
     {
         dprintk(XENLOG_ERR, "Phandle allocation overlaps Xen reserved range\n");
--
